A||N||4||bHooper||a/c compressor has an oily residue on it!||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||06-21-2001||10:31 PM||||i was working to move the a/c compressor, so that i can get that one last manifold bolt out (v6 auto), and i noticed that it has an oily residue on it.<P>is this normal?  what will happen if this persists?  is the a/c on the kaputz?<P>this is on the SE that I got from Doug Kinney, and he had converted it to 134a.<P>thanks in advance!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||06-21-2001||10:41 PM||||It could really be anything, hoop.. Does the residue seem to be around the fittings or what? <P>Here's my take on it..<BR>If you are losing oil, you will also lose freon. The freon will go first since it is a gas. Therefore, if the air is still cold I think you are okay. Of course, Murphy's Law always rules supreme. But that is how I would figure it...<P>The a/c compressor is mounted low and there is no telling where the residue came from really... it could have been from somewhere else.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||06-22-2001||12:22 AM||||wipe off your compressor and see if its leaking where the pieces fit together.  You can replace the seal.  Its in the service manual on how to do that.<P>Eventually, if it continuous to leak, you will lock up your converter.  I would try not to use the air conditioning at all, until you get it fixed.  The compressor will lock up and you wont be able to drive your car, because the belt will be unable to move any of the accessories.  waterpump, alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||06-22-2001||01:26 AM||||ok you know the ac systen has oil in it right, thin oil like 3in1.<BR>is the oil coming out the front between the pully and the compresser and runs down only that a bad seal if itis on top or all over maybe a drip from motor oil leak.<BR>but even if compresser locks up the clutch will still let the belt spin if the a/c is off.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-22-2001||09:10 PM||||Well... As stated above... clean the thing off and see where the oil is comming out at.<P>Generally, if a compressor siezes you will toast off the belt really quick. Assuming it doesn't just snap.<P>For most people a leaking shaft seal means compressor replacement. It takes special tools to remove the clutch w/o breaking anything.<P>A rear leak could be a seal or a switch. Those can be fixed but require evacing the system.<P>You also need to know just what "conversion" means to whoever actually did it. In most cases this means someone simply shoved in more oil and R134. This manytime leads to problems that don't show up right away.||||reg||1
